## Result
No confirmed client-side vulnerability. No source→HTML/exec sink with
attacker-controlled data on `/message`.
== result/client-side/console.byteplus.com/dts/region-dts-ap-southeast-1/route-report.md
== result/client-side/console.byteplus.com/datatester/route-report.md
## Results (all Not Vulnerable / Unresolved, none confirmed)
- Kura microfrontend runtime (75131) uses `eval`/`new Function` on module code,
  but module URLs come from the server-provided registry manifest, not from
  query/localStorage. Not attacker-controlled.
== result/client-side/console.byteplus.com/iam/route-report.md
## Summary
No client-side vulnerability confirmed. The only weak-origin postMessage
listener (`yn` in the TEA/DataRangers AB SDK, `usermono.js`) does not give an
attacker control of any sink: the sole dispatch type it registers
== result/client-side/console.byteplus.com/datarangers/route-report.md
## Results
- Sinks `dangerouslySetInnerHTML` on notification `thumb_content`/`message_text`
  are server-controlled; notification list empty; no write/delivery primitive
  found. **Unresolved** (would need an attacker-controlled notification body).
